ls)\n9. **Scores**: Soundness (1-5), Excitement (1-5), Reproducibility (1-5), Overall Assessment (1-5, half-point), Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Evaluate whether this paper makes a sufficient intellectual contribution for the venue, given that it uses existing models and applies existing statistical tools to characterize a phenomenon.","uuid":"37c48b82-e94a-4169-a305-618818a4ead6","session_id":"e9550a7c-c680-4184-8420-886a108eb62d"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an EMNLP 2026 submission. Your focus areas are **Novelty** and **Related Work**. You must provide a blinded, independent review.\n\n## Venue: EMNLP 2026 (via ARR)\nScoring scale (Overall Assessment, half-point increments 1–5):\n- 5 = Consider for Award (top 2.5%)\n- 4.5 = Borderline Award\n- 4 = Conference (acceptable for main conference)\n- 3.5 = Borderline Conference\n- 3 = Findings (acceptable for Findings)\n- 2.5 = Borderline Findings\n- 2 = Resubmit next cycle\n- 1.5 = Resubmit after next cycle\n- 1 = Do not resubmit\nAcceptance rate: ~21% for Main. Calibrate accordingly.\nDimensions: Soundness (1–5), Excitement (1–5), Reproducibility (1–5).\n\n## Paper summary\nTitle: \"Per-Example Analysis of Attention-Induced Convergence in Hybrid Recurrent Architectures\"\nAnonymous ACL submission, 8 pages main text + 2 pages appendix/references.\n\nThe paper analyzes a public model zoo of 72+ hybrid recurrent-attention models (GatedDeltaNet, RetNet, HGRN2, Mamba2-hybrid) across 5 attention ratios and 2 scales (340M, 1.3B). Three main claims:\n1. **Task-dependent convergence**: Adding attention reduces cross-architecture performance variance on retrieval-dependent tasks (LAMBADA ρ=−1.0, StoryCloze ρ=−1.0, exact permutation p=0.017 each, BH-FDR adj p=0.042 on pre-specified retrieval subset m=5), but not on reasoning tasks.\n2. **Scale-dependent convergence**: Monotonic at 1.3B, non-monotonic rebound at 340M attributed to capacity bottleneck. Rebound samples 73% in top difficulty quartile.\n3. **Per-example divergence under aggregate convergence**: Despite aggregate convergence, attention affects nearly non-overlapping sample subsets across architectures (Jaccard < 0.10). Within-architecture, neither surface features nor baseline perplexity predict helped/hurt. Cross-architecture Kruskal-Wallis p=0.0005 for hurt samples.\n\nAdditional details:\n- Mamba2-hybrid added as 4th architecture\n- Attention pattern analysis: mean r=0.36 (descriptive only)\n- Co-evolution of convergence and divergence quantified (ρ=−1.0)\n- Three-layer multiple comparison correction\n- Paper frames itself as \"community analysis paper\" analogous to medical meta-analyses\n- Related work covers: hybrid architectures (Wang et al. 2025 as primary comparison), underspecification (D'Amour et al. 2022), ensemble diversity (Krogh & Vedelsby 1995; Kuncheva & Whitaker 2003), dataset cartography (Swayamdipta et al. 2020; Ethayarajh et al. 2022), attention analysis (Vaswani et al. 2017)\n- Wang et al. (2025) differentiation: 5 dimensions (quantification, per-example, scale mechanism, taxonomy, multi-benchmark)\n\n## Your task\nProduce a structured review covering:\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Novelty assessment** — evaluate: (a) how novel is the \"community analysis\" contribution model for *ACL venues? (b) is the per-example decomposition of convergence genuinely new or a straightforward application of known tools (Jaccard, Kruskal-Wallis)? (c) does the retrieval-demand taxonomy offer predictive value beyond post-hoc labeling? (d) how well does the paper differentiate from Wang et al. (2025)?\n3. **Related work assessment** — evaluate: (a) completeness of related work coverage, (b) are there missing references (e.g., mechanistic interpretability work on attention heads, recent hybrid architecture papers beyond those cited, ensemble pruning literature)? (c) is the underspecification framing appropriate and well-connected?\n4. **Strengths** (bulleted, 4-6 items)\n5. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, 4-6 items, be specific and actionable)\n6. **Questions for authors** (3-5 items)\n7. **Novelty-specific concerns** — does this meet the bar for EMNLP 2026 Main? Findings? What would elevate it?\n8. **Minor issues** (formatting, clarity, missing details)\n9. **Scores**: Soundness (1-5), Excitement (1-5), Reproducibility (1-5), Overall Assessment (1-5, half-point), Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Evaluate whether this paper makes a sufficient intellectual contribution for the venue, given that it uses existing models and applies existing statistical tools to characterize a phenomenon."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01GnHFXk6S1ZBkRtDjBgc5n2","session_id":"e9550a7c-c680-4184-8420-886a108eb62d","uuid":"bf662f92-8079-46b0-8f03-d3bc1217d4f2","timestamp":"2026-05-19T15:25:38.407Z"}
