{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VSzXghkAUfr13Hqi39zQ2V","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,twocolumn]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n3\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n4\t\\usepackage{times}\n5\t\\usepackage{geometry}\n6\t\\geometry{a4paper,margin=2.5cm}\n7\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n8\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n9\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n10\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n11\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n12\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n13\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n14\t\\usepackage{tabularx}\n15\t\\usepackage{caption}\n16\t\\captionsetup{font=small}\n17\t\n18\t\\title{Task-Dependent Convergence and Per-Example Divergence\\\\in Attention-Augmented Linear Recurrent Models}\n19\t\n20\t\\author{\n21\tAnonymous Submission to EMNLP 2026\n22\t}\n23\t\\date{}\n24\t\n25\t\\begin{document}\n26\t\\maketitle\n27\t\n28\t\\begin{abstract}\n29\tHybrid architectures that interleave linear recurrent layers with full attention are increasingly adopted, yet how attention shapes cross-architecture behavioral diversity remains poorly understood at the per-example level.\n30\tUsing the public model zoo of \\citet{wang2025mamba} (72 pretrained models spanning three linear recurrent architectures at two scales), we provide a systematic per-example analysis of attention's equalizing effect.\n31\tWe report three findings.\n32\t(1)~Aggregate cross-architecture convergence is task-dependent: on the retrieval-heavy LAMBADA benchmark, adding attention reduces cross-architecture standard deviation by 79\\% ($\\rho{=}{-}1.0$, permutation $p{=}0.017$), with directionally consistent trends on BoolQ ($\\rho{=}{-}0.8$) and StoryCloze ($\\rho{=}{-}0.7$), but no convergence on reasoning-heavy tasks.\n33\t(2)~Convergence requires sufficient model capacity: it is monotonically complete at 1.3B parameters but exhibits a non-monotonic rebound at 340M, disproportionately affecting longer-context, higher-difficulty examples.\n34\t(3)~Despite aggregate convergence, attention acts on nearly non-overlapping sample subsets across architectures (pairwise Jaccard~$<$~0.10), an instance of underspecification with practical implications for architecture selection.\n35\tSurface features and baseline perplexity do not predict which samples are affected within an architecture, yet cross-architecture comparison reveals systematically different difficulty profiles (Kruskal--Wallis $p{=}0.0005$).\n36\t\\end{abstract}\n37\t\n38\t\\section{Introduction}\n39\t\n40\tLinear recurrent models---including RetNet \\citep{sun2023retentive}, HGRN2 \\citep{qin2024hgrn2}, GatedDeltaNet \\citep{yang2024gated}, RWKV \\citep{peng2023rwkv}, and Mamba \\citep{gu2023mamba}---offer sub-quadratic sequence processing and have emerged as practical alternatives to Transformers \\citep{vaswani2017attention}.\n41\tA widespread design pattern interleaves these recurrent layers with standard attention layers, creating hybrid architectures that trade off efficiency and expressiveness \\citep{lieber2024jamba,de2024griffin}.\n42\t\n43\t\\citet{wang2025mamba} recently released a comprehensive model zoo of 72 hybrid models, systematically varying the architecture backbone (GatedDeltaNet, RetNet, HGRN2), attention ratio (0/24 to 24/24 layers), and scale (340M, 1.3B parameters).\n44\tThey observe qualitatively that ``models tend to converge in performance as attention ratio increases.''\n45\tThis observation raises a natural question: \\emph{does aggregate convergence reflect convergence at the per-example level, or does it mask architecture-specific behavioral patterns?}\n46\t\n47\tThis question is motivated by well-established phenomena in the broader machine learning literature.\n48\tEnsemble diversity theory \\citep{krogh1994neural,kuncheva2003measures} shows that models with similar aggregate accuracy can disagree substantially on individual examples---indeed, such disagreement is the basis for ensemble gains.\n49\tThe underspecification framework of \\citet{damour2022underspecification} formalizes how equivalent aggregate performance can mask prediction-level differences, and dataset cartography \\citep{swayamdipta2020dataset} documents per-example difficulty variation across training runs.\n50\tWhether these phenomena manifest when attention is systematically added to linear recurrent models has not been quantified."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01GGaGF3qmsmE8jirs1mTivm","session_id":"e9550a7c-c680-4184-8420-886a108eb62d","uuid":"c5554203-cc5a-4461-aa73-dcf0b59d3ec8","timestamp":"2026-05-19T15:26:16.588Z"}
