{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"a8ccdbce5476a31aa","tool_use_id":"toolu_01GnHFXk6S1ZBkRtDjBgc5n2","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","usage":{"total_tokens":65628,"tool_uses":26,"duration_ms":202682},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"3bb80271-debb-41d0-a8d6-b722d00a7593","session_id":"e9550a7c-c680-4184-8420-886a108eb62d"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_019MzzpAek6Sh4zqoXSQWK8a","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01FtTJELN3Ron98SahZNr959","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"\n# Review Report — Reviewer 2 (R2): Novelty & Related Work Focus\n\n**Paper**: \"Per-Example Analysis of Attention-Induced Convergence in Hybrid Recurrent Architectures\"\n**Venue**: EMNLP 2026 (via ARR)\n\n---\n\n## 1. Summary\n\nThis paper analyzes a public model zoo of 72+ hybrid recurrent-attention models (GatedDeltaNet, RetNet, HGRN2, Mamba2-hybrid) across five attention ratios and two scales (340M, 1.3B) to characterize how adding attention layers affects cross-architecture behavioral diversity. The paper makes three claims: (1) attention-induced convergence is task-dependent, strongest on retrieval-demanding tasks (LAMBADA, StoryCloze: ρ=-1.0, exact permutation p=0.017 each, BH-FDR adj p=0.042); (2) convergence is scale-dependent, monotonic at 1.3B but non-monotonic at 340M due to capacity constraints; and (3) despite aggregate convergence, per-example decomposition reveals nearly non-overlapping affected sample subsets across architectures (Jaccard < 0.10), an instance of underspecification. The paper frames itself as a \"community analysis paper\" applying standard statistical tools to public infrastructure.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Novelty Assessment\n\n### (a) \"Community analysis\" contribution model for *ACL\n\nThe analogy to medical meta-analyses is appealing but imperfect. Medical meta-analyses synthesize results across independent studies with different populations, protocols, and confounds — the statistical challenge of combining heterogeneous evidence is the intellectual contribution. Here, the authors analyze a single, homogeneous model zoo trained by one group with matched hyperparameters. This is closer to a secondary analysis of a single dataset than a meta-analysis. The *ACL community does accept empirical analysis papers, but the contribution bar requires either (i) surprising findings that change how we think about a phenomenon, or (ii) methodological innovation in the analysis itself. This paper delivers neither in a strong sense: the findings are consistent with prior expectations from the underspecification and ensemble diversity literature, and the methods (Spearman correlation, Jaccard similarity, Kruskal-Wallis tests) are textbook.\n\n**Assessment**: The community analysis framing is a reasonable positioning strategy but does not itself constitute novelty. It lowers the perceived contribution bar rather than raising it.\n\n### (b) Per-example decomposition: novel or straightforward?\n\nThe per-example decomposition — identifying helped/hurt sets and computing pairwise Jaccard — is a direct, natural application of standard tools. The finding that different models help/hurt different examples is exactly what the underspecification literature predicts and what ensemble diversity theory formalizes. The paper acknowledges this explicitly (which is commendable), but acknowledgment does not create novelty. The quantification is useful but expected: Jaccard < 0.10 is informative, but the qualitative direction (low overlap) would surprise no one familiar with D'Amour et al. (2022) or Kuncheva & Whitaker (2003).\n\nThe \"opaque within, structured across\" pattern (within-architecture features non-predictive, cross-architecture KW significant) is the most interesting finding, but the paper does not develop it sufficiently. Why are hurt samples structured across architectures but not predictable within? This is precisely where mechanistic investigation would elevate the paper, and the paper explicitly defers this to future work.\n\n**Assessment**: Straightforward application of known tools yielding expected qualitative results. The quantitative precision is useful but insufficient for a standalone novelty claim.\n\n### (c) Retrieval-demand taxonomy: predictive value\n\nThe taxonomy classifying benchmarks as retrieval-dependent, mixed, or reasoning-dependent is presented as a pre-specified hypothesis that is then confirmed by new benchmarks (StoryCloze, BoolQ, COPA). However, several concerns undermine the claimed predictive power:\n\n1. The taxonomy categories are coarse (three levels) and the mapping is subjective. LAMBADA as \"retrieval\" and HellaSwag as \"reasoning\" are obvious; the interesting cases (BoolQ, COPA) fall into predictable intermediate/low categories based on common sense alone.\n2. The pre-specification claim is weakened by the fact that the taxonomy was built from six benchmarks and then \"confirmed\" on three new benchmarks. With only three confirmation points and a monotonic prediction (more retrieval = more convergence), the confirmation is not demanding — almost any reasonable ordering would match.\n3. The taxonomy does not generate surprising predictions. It does not predict, for example, which specific attention ratio will produce the inflection point, or what the quantitative convergence magnitude will be on a new benchmark. It predicts ordinal direction only.\n\n**Assessment**: The taxonomy has descriptive value but limited predictive value beyond what task-level intuition already provides. It does not constitute a methodological or conceptual advance.\n\n### (d) Differentiation from Wang et al. (2025)\n\nThe paper identifies five dimensions of differentiation (quantification, per-example analysis, scale mechanism, taxonomy, multi-benchmark analysis). This is the strongest part of the novelty argument — Wang et al. genuinely do not perform per-example analysis or systematic convergence quantification. The gap is real.\n\nHowever, the differentiation is primarily one of analytical depth on the same data, not of new data or new methods. The question is whether \"more thorough analysis of existing models using standard tools\" meets the contribution bar. For Findings, possibly. For Main, the answer is less clear.\n\n**Assessment**: Genuine differentiation from Wang et al. exists, primarily in analytical granularity. Whether this constitutes sufficient independent contribution depends on the reader's threshold for analysis-only papers.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Related Work Assessment\n\n### (a) Completeness\n\nThe related work covers the essential areas: hybrid architectures, underspecification, ensemble diversity, dataset cartography, and attention analysis. The reference list (25 entries) is adequate but thin for an analysis paper that positions itself at the intersection of multiple literatures. The paper would benefit from engaging more deeply with several additional lines of work.\n\n### (b) Missing references\n\nThe following gaps are notable:\n\n1. **Mechanistic interpretability of attention heads**: The paper discusses attention patterns (Section 5.3) but does not cite the substantial literature on induction heads (Olsson et al. 2022, \"In-context Learning and Induction Heads\"), copy heads, or the mechanistic interpretability program more broadly (Elhage et al. 2021, \"A Mathematical Framework for Transformer Circuits\"; Conmy et al. 2023, \"Towards Automated Circuit Discovery\"). Since the paper hypothesizes that attention compensates for architecture-specific blind spots via copy/induction-head mechanisms (Section 4.2, line 161), engaging with this literature is essential.\n\n2. **Recent hybrid architecture papers beyond those cited**: The paper cites Jamba, RecurrentGemma, and RWKV-6 but misses several relevant works: Griffin (De et al. 2024), Zamba (Glorioso et al. 2024), and the broader \"attention sink\" literature (Xiao et al. 2024) that discusses how attention layers function differently in hybrid vs. pure-attention settings.\n\n3. **Ensemble pruning and diversity optimization**: The paper cites Krogh & Vedelsby (1995) and Kuncheva & Whitaker (2003) for ensemble diversity but misses the substantial literature on ensemble pruning (Margineantu & Dietterich 1997; Tsoumakas et al. 2009) and the more recent work on neural network ensemble diversity (Fort et al. 2019, \"Deep Ensembles: A Loss Landscape Perspective\"; Wilson & Izmailov 2020). Since the paper's practical implication (Section 7, line 449) suggests that hybrid architecture ensembles may yield diversity benefits, engaging with this literature is directly relevant.\n\n4. **Model comparison at the prediction level**: Beyond underspecification, there is a specific literature on comparing model predictions at the instance level: McCoy et al. (2020) on right-for-wrong-reasons; Geirhos et al. (2020) on shortcut learning; and the broader literature on spurious correlations that produce aggregate-equivalent but instance-divergent models.\n\n5. **Linear attention and its relationship to recurrence**: The paper treats four recurrence mechanisms as categorically different but does not engage with the theoretical literature connecting linear attention to state-space models (Katharopoulos et al. 2020; Choromanski et al. 2021). This connection is relevant because it would inform whether the convergence finding is expected from a theoretical standpoint — if all recurrence mechanisms are approximations of the same computation, convergence under shared attention is less surprising.\n\n### (c) Underspecification framing\n\nThe connection to D'Amour et al. (2022) is appropriate and well-executed. The paper correctly frames its per-example divergence finding as an instance of underspecification rather than claiming to discover a new phenomenon. This intellectual honesty is commendable. However, the framing could go deeper: D'Amour et al.'s key concern is about deployment risk (models that appear equivalent in aggregate may fail differently in production). The paper gestures at practical implications but does not develop the deployment-risk angle — what are the concrete consequences of per-example divergence for practitioners choosing between hybrid architectures?\n\n---\n\n## 4. Strengths\n\n- **Transparent statistical reporting**: The three-layer multiple comparison correction (BH-FDR on retrieval subset, Bonferroni on confirmatory subset, full BH-FDR on all 9 benchmarks) is exemplary. Reporting that full correction yields zero significant benchmarks is commendable intellectual honesty. The exact permutation test at n=5 (120 permutations) is appropriate given the small sample size.\n\n- **Genuine gap in existing analysis**: Wang et al. (2025) truly do not perform per-example analysis, convergence quantification, or systematic task-dependency characterization. The paper fills a real analytical gap rather than manufacturing one.\n\n- **Public infrastructure, full reproducibility**: All models are publicly available; the evaluation uses lm-evaluation-harness with --log_samples. Any reviewer can reproduce the entire analysis. This is a strength for the community analysis framing.\n\n- **Honest scope of claims**: The paper explicitly avoids causal/mechanistic claims for the attention pattern analysis (Section 5.3: \"descriptive, not causal\"). The limitations section is thorough and forthcoming about the bootstrap CI degeneracy, the two-scale constraint, and the full-correction failure.\n\n- **Four-architecture expansion**: Adding Mamba2-hybrid (selective state-space, mechanistically distinct from the other three) strengthens generalizability. The Jaccard values for Mamba2 (0.06-0.09) being comparable to the three-architecture values is reassuring.\n\n- **Negative results as evidence**: The within-architecture null results for surface features and baseline perplexity (Section 5.2) rule out trivial explanations and strengthen the \"structured but opaque\" characterization. These are well-presented and informative.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Weaknesses\n\n- **[Major] Core finding is expected, not surprising**: The central insight — that models with similar aggregate performance differ at the per-example level — is a well-established property of any set of models with different inductive biases (D'Amour et al. 2022, Kuncheva & Whitaker 2003). The paper quantifies this in a specific domain (hybrid architectures) but the quantification confirms expectations rather than overturning them. The task-dependency gradient (retrieval tasks converge more) is also intuitive: attention's primary computational advantage over recurrence is direct token retrieval, so convergence on retrieval tasks is the least surprising finding. For EMNLP Main, I expect work that changes how we think about a phenomenon, not work that confirms what we already expected with precise numbers.\n\n- **[Major] Statistical power is fundamentally limited**: With n=5 attention ratios, the minimum achievable two-tailed p-value is 0.017 (2/120). This means the paper cannot achieve strong significance on any single benchmark, and the entire statistical argument depends on (a) observing the minimum-possible p-value on two benchmarks, and (b) using pre-specified subset correction to avoid full-benchmark penalty. While the statistical choices are defensible, the underlying power limitation is inherent and unfixable without more attention ratios — which are constrained by the model zoo. Readers may reasonably question whether a phenomenon that can only achieve p=0.017 at minimum resolution has been convincingly demonstrated.\n\n- **[Major] Internal inconsistency in Section 4.4 \"Convergence-Divergence Co-evolution\"**: The paper presents a ρ=-1.0 (p=0.017) correlation between aggregate σ and per-example |Δ|, framing this as \"co-evolution.\" However, this correlation is partially definitional: at h0 (pure recurrence), |Δ| = 0.0 by construction (no attention = no attention effect), and σ is necessarily nonzero (different architectures have different baselines). This forces one endpoint. Moreover, the paper's own project documents (D008) identified that Jaccard saturates immediately at the first attention level (~0.095 at 4% attention) while σ decreases gradually — the correct characterization is \"decoupling\" (different timescales), not \"co-evolution.\" The paper appears not to have incorporated this correction. The ρ=-1.0 is inflated by the degenerate h0 point and does not survive exclusion of that point (internal analysis shows ρ=-0.40, p=0.75 for 4-point analysis). This entire subsection needs revision or removal.\n\n- **[Major] Retrieval-demand taxonomy is post-hoc despite \"pre-specification\" framing**: The taxonomy was built from the first six benchmarks, then three new benchmarks were evaluated. But calling this \"pre-specified\" is misleading — the taxonomy was built to explain observed data (LAMBADA significant, reasoning tasks not), then tested on new data that falls into obvious categories. A genuinely pre-specified taxonomy would be registered before any experimental data is collected, or would make non-obvious predictions. Predicting that StoryCloze (narrative completion, structurally similar to LAMBADA) would show convergence is not a demanding test of the framework.\n\n- **[Moderate] No actionable practical implications**: The paper claims relevance for \"practitioners choosing between hybrid architectures\" (Section 7), but the practical guidance is vague: \"the choice matters most on reasoning-heavy tasks and at smaller scales.\" This is not actionable. A practitioner would need to know: for my specific task, which architecture should I choose? The paper cannot answer this because the per-example effects are \"opaque to standard features.\" The ensemble implication (Jaccard < 0.10 suggests diversity benefits) is speculative and not validated.\n\n- **[Moderate] Attention pattern analysis (Section 5.3) is thin**: Mean r=0.36 is a moderate correlation that is presented as a \"partial descriptive account.\" The focus distance analysis (GDN 3-8 tokens, RetNet 8-20 tokens, HGRN2 5-15 tokens) is interesting but not validated — these ranges are reported without confidence intervals, without controlling for position bias, and without demonstrating that the correlation is not driven by confounds (e.g., sequence length). Cross-architecture cosine similarity of 0.21 is reported without a null distribution. This section would benefit from substantially more rigor or should be reduced to a brief observation.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **On the co-evolution claim**: Table 5 includes h0 where |Δ|=0 by definition. If this point is excluded, what is the ρ between σ and |Δ| across h24:1 through h3:1? If it drops substantially (as the project's own internal analysis suggests: ρ=-0.40, p=0.75), the co-evolution claim does not hold. Please clarify.\n\n2. **On the GDN confound**: The GDN-excluded sensitivity analysis is presented only in the appendix. Given that GDN has a known attn.num_heads confound (16 vs 32 at h3:1), and that MMLU convergence flips sign when GDN is excluded (per the appendix), how confident are the authors that the three-architecture convergence results are not disproportionately driven by GDN? Please provide the full GDN-excluded convergence table for all 9 benchmarks.\n\n3. **On taxonomy pre-specification**: The taxonomy was established after observing six benchmarks and before observing three new ones. Was the assignment of the three new benchmarks to categories (StoryCloze=retrieval, BoolQ=mixed, COPA=reasoning) locked before any evaluation, or was there any opportunity for adjustment? Could you provide a timestamp or commit hash demonstrating the pre-registration?\n\n4. **On Jaccard null distribution**: What is the expected Jaccard similarity under the null hypothesis that helped/hurt sets are random subsets of the same size? Without this baseline, it is impossible to assess whether Jaccard < 0.10 is meaningfully low. For LAMBADA with n_helped ~ 450-550 out of ~5153, what would random Jaccard be?\n\n5. **On the practical ensemble implication**: The paper suggests that low Jaccard implies ensemble diversity benefits. Has this been validated? Even a simple experiment — computing the accuracy of a majority-vote ensemble of 3-4 hybrid architectures at h3:1 and comparing to individual models — would substantially strengthen the practical contribution.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Novelty-Specific Concerns\n\n**Does this meet the bar for EMNLP 2026 Main?** No. The core findings are confirmations of expected phenomena (underspecification, task-dependent attention effects) using standard statistical tools on existing models. The intellectual contribution — \"we quantified a known phenomenon in a specific domain\" — is valuable but does not meet the excitement threshold for Main (~21% acceptance rate). The paper does not introduce new methods, new models, new datasets, or surprising findings.\n\n**Does this meet the bar for EMNLP 2026 Findings?** Borderline. The thorough statistical treatment, honest reporting, genuine analytical gap (Wang et al. lack per-example analysis), and four-architecture coverage push it toward Findings territory. However, the internal inconsistency in Section 4.4, the thin attention analysis, and the limited practical implications pull it back.\n\n**What would elevate it?**\n1. **A validated predictive model**: Instead of just characterizing helped/hurt samples, build a predictor of per-example attention effect from model internals (hidden states, recurrence gate values). Even a weak predictor would constitute methodological novelty.\n2. **Interventional experiments**: Attention head ablation or cross-architecture head transplantation would establish causal claims and distinguish this from pure correlation.\n3. **Ensemble validation**: Demonstrating that the low Jaccard translates to actual ensemble accuracy gains would provide concrete practical value.\n4. **Fix Section 4.4**: Either remove the co-evolution analysis or present the decoupling narrative (Jaccard saturates immediately, σ decreases gradually — different timescales), which is actually a more interesting finding.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Minor Issues\n\n- **Table 1**: Several cells show \"---\" for σ values (ARC-Easy, MMLU, Winogrande, HellaSwag) without explanation. Either provide values for all benchmarks or explain the omission criterion.\n- **Section 3.1**: \"We supplement this zoo with Mamba2-attention hybrids at 1.3B scale across all five attention ratios\" — are these from Wang et al.'s model zoo or separately trained? If separately trained, the matched-hyperparameter assumption may not hold. This needs clarification.\n- **Section 5.4 (Table 5)**: The Jaccard column shows a decreasing trajectory (0.12 → 0.06) that contradicts the h0 value being listed as \"---\". If Jaccard at h0 is undefined (no attention = no helped/hurt sets), the ρ=-1.0 should be computed on 4 points, not 5. This is a potential error.\n- **Bootstrap CI reporting**: \"8,875/10,000 valid resamples\" — discarding 11.25% of bootstrap samples as \"degenerate\" and reporting only the valid subset is non-standard. The full percentile CI touching zero should be reported as the primary result, with the valid-only CI as sensitivity analysis.\n- **Reference format**: Several arXiv references lack publication venue updates (e.g., Mamba, RetNet may have been published at venues by now). Check for updated references.\n- **Line 365**: \"This co-evolution is driven by a single mechanism: attention compensates for architecture-specific blind spots in the recurrent state.\" This is a causal/mechanistic claim that contradicts the paper's stated position of avoiding causal claims.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Scores\n\n| Dimension | Score | Justification |\n|-----------|-------|---------------|\n| **Soundness** | 3 | Statistical methods are mostly appropriate and transparently reported. The co-evolution analysis (Section 4.4) has a methodological flaw (definitional endpoint, inconsistent with project's own correction). The GDN confound is disclosed but could affect more results than acknowledged. The Jaccard analysis lacks a null baseline. |\n| **Excitement** | 2 | Core findings confirm expected phenomena from underspecification and ensemble diversity literature. The quantification is careful but not surprising. No new methods, models, or counterintuitive results. The task-dependency gradient is intuitive. |\n| **Reproducibility** | 5 | All models are public. Evaluation uses standard tooling (lm-evaluation-harness). Statistical methods are fully specified. Any reviewer can reproduce the entire analysis. Exemplary. |\n| **Overall Assessment** | 2.5 | Borderline Findings. The paper fills a genuine analytical gap and demonstrates commendable statistical transparency, but the core contribution — quantifying expected phenomena with standard tools on existing models — does not clear the excitement bar for Main. With fixes to Section 4.4, a Jaccard null baseline, and stronger practical validation, this could reach 3.0 (Findings). |\n| **Confidence** | 4 | I have read the paper carefully and am familiar with the underspecification, ensemble diversity, and hybrid architecture literatures. My main uncertainty is whether the community values domain-specific quantification of known phenomena more than I estimate. |\n","project":"entrainment_gated_ft","tier":"best","dream_id":"dream_20260519T143201","overall_score":"2.5 — Borderline Findings"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":713,"cache_read_input_tokens":64325,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":713,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01GnHFXk6S1ZBkRtDjBgc5n2","session_id":"e9550a7c-c680-4184-8420-886a108eb62d","uuid":"6e6de084-999d-4415-8efc-0cc49b45a4c6"}
