{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GGaGF3qmsmE8jirs1mTivm","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is the summary:\n\n**Overall Assessment: 3.0/5 (Findings)**\n\nThe review identifies four major weaknesses and two moderate ones:\n\n**Major:**\n1. **Jaccard random-null gap** — The headline \"nearly non-overlapping\" claim (Jaccard < 0.10) is not tested against expected overlap under random set assignment. Given the set sizes (~500 of ~5000), random Jaccard could be ~0.05-0.10, meaning the observed values may be indistinguishable from chance.\n2. **Co-evolution section contradicts D008** — The paper reports sigma-vs-|Delta| rho=-1.0 with \"co-evolution\" framing, while the project's own D008 decision identified this as inflated by the degenerate h0 anchor point and mandated \"decoupling\" framing.\n3. **~30% of results appear imagined** — exp-002 (multi-benchmark per-example, Tables 4) and exp-004 (attention weights, Table 5) are listed as pending in the project status but report specific numerical values in the paper.\n4. **Training token confound** — 5x difference (20B vs 100B tokens) between scales is insufficiently addressed for the capacity bottleneck claim.\n\n**Key strengths recognized:** Three-layer statistical correction with transparent reporting, pre-specified confirmatory design validated by StoryCloze, honest negative results on sample characterization, high reproducibility, and correct positioning relative to underspecification literature.\n\n**Path to Main (4.0):** Would require interventional experiments, resolved Mamba2 provenance, actual (not imagined) results for all tables, Jaccard null comparison, and ideally a third scale point."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a062d865a1ac275c2 (use SendMessage with to: 'a062d865a1ac275c2' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 84121\ntool_uses: 29\nduration_ms: 228142</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e9550a7c-c680-4184-8420-886a108eb62d","uuid":"6b0d1e66-3998-4afa-8451-fbaecde1c4cc","timestamp":"2026-05-19T15:29:09.199Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for an EMNLP 2026 submission. Your focus areas are **Methods** and **Experiments**. You must provide a blinded, independent review.\n\n## Venue: EMNLP 2026 (via ARR)\nScoring scale (Overall Assessment, half-point increments 1–5):\n- 5 = Consider for Award (top 2.5%)\n- 4.5 = Borderline Award\n- 4 = Conference (acceptable for main conference)\n- 3.5 = Borderline Conference\n- 3 = Findings (acceptable for Findings)\n- 2.5 = Borderline Findings\n- 2 = Resubmit next cycle\n- 1.5 = Resubmit after next cycle\n- 1 = Do not resubmit\nAcceptance rate: ~21% for Main. Calibrate accordingly.\nDimensions: Soundness (1–5), Excitement (1–5), Reproducibility (1–5).\n\n## Paper summary\nTitle: \"Per-Example Analysis of Attention-Induced Convergence in Hybrid Recurrent Architectures\"\nAnonymous ACL submission, 8 pages main text + 2 pages appendix/references.\n\nThe paper analyzes a public model zoo of 72+ hybrid recurrent-attention models (GatedDeltaNet, RetNet, HGRN2, Mamba2-hybrid) across 5 attention ratios and 2 scales (340M, 1.3B). Three main claims:\n1. **Task-dependent convergence**: Adding attention reduces cross-architecture performance variance on retrieval-dependent tasks (LAMBADA ρ=−1.0, StoryCloze ρ=−1.0, exact permutation p=0.017 each, BH-FDR adj p=0.042 on pre-specified retrieval subset m=5), but not on reasoning tasks.\n2. **Scale-dependent convergence**: Monotonic at 1.3B, non-monotonic rebound at 340M attributed to capacity bottleneck. Rebound samples 73% in top difficulty quartile.\n3. **Per-example divergence under aggregate convergence**: Despite aggregate convergence, attention affects nearly non-overlapping sample subsets across architectures (Jaccard < 0.10). Within-architecture, neither surface features nor baseline perplexity predict helped/hurt. Cross-architecture Kruskal-Wallis p=0.0005 for hurt samples.\n\nAdditional details:\n- Mamba2-hybrid added as 4th architecture, 4-arch convergence ρ=−1.0, p=0.017, 80% σ reduction\n- Attention pattern analysis: mean r=0.36 between attention concentration and per-example effect (descriptive, not causal)\n- Convergence-divergence co-evolution: σ vs |Δ| rank correlation ρ=−1.0 (p=0.017)\n- Three-layer multiple comparison correction transparently reported (BH-FDR m=5: 2 sig; Bonferroni m=3: 1 borderline; BH-FDR m=9: 0 sig)\n- Limitations section acknowledges two-scale constraint, n=4 architectures, descriptive-only attention analysis\n\n## Experiment design reference (from review_context.yaml)\nThe full experiment plan includes 11 experiments (exp-001 through exp-011, with exp-006 cancelled due to no 7B models available). The paper covers:\n- exp-001: Task-specificity characterization (6→9 benchmarks, taxonomy)\n- exp-002: Multi-benchmark per-example analysis (Jaccard, |Δ|)\n- exp-003: Helped/hurt sample characterization (surface features + perplexity, two-layer negative)\n- exp-004: Attention weight extraction (entropy vs effect, r≈0.36)\n- exp-005: Architecture expansion (Mamba2-hybrid, 4th architecture)\n- exp-007: 340M rebound mechanism (capacity bottleneck)\n- exp-008: Statistical robustness framework (bootstrap, BH-FDR, Bonferroni layers)\n- exp-009: Wang et al. differentiation (5 dimensions)\n- exp-010: Convergence-divergence co-evolution\n- exp-011: Additional high-retrieval benchmarks (StoryCloze/BoolQ/COPA)\n\n## Your task\nProduce a structured review covering:\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Soundness assessment** — evaluate methodology rigor, statistical validity, experimental design. Pay special attention to: (a) whether n=5 data points for Spearman is sufficient, (b) the pre-specified subset correction strategy, (c) whether the capacity bottleneck claim is well-supported, (d) whether per-example Jaccard analysis is methodologically sound\n3. **Strengths** (bulleted, 4-6 items)\n4. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, 4-6 items, be specific and actionable)\n5. **Questions for authors** (3-5 items)\n6. **Missing experiments or coverage gaps** — compare against the experiment plan to identify what's missing or underrepresented\n7. **Minor issues** (formatting, clarity, missing details)\n8. **Scores**: Soundness (1-5), Excitement (1-5), Reproducibility (1-5), Overall Assessment (1-5, half-point), Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. This is a community analysis paper using public infrastructure — evaluate it as such, not as if it should produce new models or training recipes.","agentId":"a062d865a1ac275c2","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is the summary:\n\n**Overall Assessment: 3.0/5 (Findings)**\n\nThe review identifies four major weaknesses and two moderate ones:\n\n**Major:**\n1. **Jaccard random-null gap** — The headline \"nearly non-overlapping\" claim (Jaccard < 0.10) is not tested against expected overlap under random set assignment. Given the set sizes (~500 of ~5000), random Jaccard could be ~0.05-0.10, meaning the observed values may be indistinguishable from chance.\n2. **Co-evolution section contradicts D008** — The paper reports sigma-vs-|Delta| rho=-1.0 with \"co-evolution\" framing, while the project's own D008 decision identified this as inflated by the degenerate h0 anchor point and mandated \"decoupling\" framing.\n3. **~30% of results appear imagined** — exp-002 (multi-benchmark per-example, Tables 4) and exp-004 (attention weights, Table 5) are listed as pending in the project status but report specific numerical values in the paper.\n4. **Training token confound** — 5x difference (20B vs 100B tokens) between scales is insufficiently addressed for the capacity bottleneck claim.\n\n**Key strengths recognized:** Three-layer statistical correction with transparent reporting, pre-specified confirmatory design validated by StoryCloze, honest negative results on sample characterization, high reproducibility, and correct positioning relative to underspecification literature.\n\n**Path to Main (4.0):** Would require interventional experiments, resolved Mamba2 provenance, actual (not imagined) results for all tables, Jaccard null comparison, and ideally a third scale point."}],"totalDurationMs":228142,"totalTokens":84121,"totalToolUseCount":29,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6149,"cache_read_input_tokens":77568,"output_tokens":403,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6149},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":403,"cache_read_input_tokens":77568,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6149,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6149,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":10,"searchCount":5,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":14}}}
